.TH PAPERWEIGHT\-EXPAND\-HOME 8 "2026-06-19" "paperweight-chromebook 0.1.12" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
paperweight\-expand\-home \- migrate /home to an SD card
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B paperweight\-expand\-home
.RI [ device ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B paperweight\-expand\-home
partitions and formats an SD card as a single ext4 partition, copies
the existing
.I /home
tree to it, and adds a UUID-based entry to
.I /etc/fstab
so that
.I /home
mounts from the SD card on subsequent boots.
.PP
On supported Chromebook models the internal eMMC is
.I /dev/mmcblk0
and the SD reader appears as
.IR /dev/mmcblk1 ,
which is the default device.
Run
.BR lsblk (8)
with and without the SD card inserted to confirm the device path on
your specific model before running this script.
.PP
The optional
.I device
argument overrides the default
.IR /dev/mmcblk1 .
.PP
Must be run as root.
All existing data on the target device will be erased.
The script prompts for explicit confirmation before making any changes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I device
Block device path for the SD card (default:
.IR /dev/mmcblk1 ).
.SH STEPS
.IP 1. 4
Erase and repartition the SD card as GPT with a single Linux data partition.
.IP 2. 4
Format the partition as ext4 with label
.IR paperweight-home .
.IP 3. 4
Copy all of
.I /home
to the new partition using
.BR rsync (1).
.IP 4. 4
Append a
.B UUID=
entry for
.I /home
to
.IR /etc/fstab .
.SH DEPENDENCIES
Requires
.BR sgdisk (8),
.BR mkfs.ext4 (8),
.BR rsync (1),
and
.BR blkid (8).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lsblk (8),
.BR sgdisk (8),
.BR mkfs.ext4 (8),
.BR rsync (1),
.BR fstab (5)
